    "q-324349": {
      "dom_id": "step-q324349",
      "name": "Question 8",
      "problem": "The flight times of a particular drone model are normally distributed with a mean of $35$ minutes and a standard deviation of $4$ minutes. What is the probability that a randomly selected drone flies more than $40.08$ minutes on a single charge? **Round your answer to two decimal places.**\n\n*Hint: A table of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ is given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution.*\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.05\\,$ | $\\,0.06\\,$ | $\\,0.07\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,1.1\\,$ | $\\,0.8749\\,$ | $\\,0.8770\\,$ | $\\,0.8790\\,$ |\n| $\\,1.2\\,$ | $\\,0.8944\\,$ | $\\,0.8962\\,$ | $\\,0.8980\\,$ |\n| $\\,1.3\\,$ | $\\,0.9115\\,$ | $\\,0.9131\\,$ | $\\,0.9147\\,$ |\n\nThe probability that a randomly selected drone flies more than $40.08$ minutes on a single charge is approximately\n{{field-1}}.",
      "worked_solution": "Let $X$ represent the drone flight time (in minutes). Then we have $X∼N(35,{4}^{2}).$\n\nWe want to find $P(X>40.08).$ Thus, we convert $X$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X>40.08) & =P(Z>\\frac{40.08-35}{4}) \\\\  & =P(Z>1.27) \\\\  & =1-P(Z\\le 1.27) \\\\  & =1-Φ(1.27)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the $z$-table, we see that\n\n$$\n1-Φ(1.27)=1-0.8980≈0.10.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability that a randomly selected drone flies more than $40.08$ minutes on a single charge is approximately $0.10.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
