    "q-94697": {
      "dom_id": "step-q94697",
      "name": "Question 11",
      "problem": "In a factory, soda cans are filled with a mean volume of $12$ ounces of soda. However, $1%$ of cans contain no more than $11.5$ ounces of soda. Assuming that the volume of soda in each can is normally distributed, find the standard deviation of the volume of soda in a can, rounded to $2$ decimal places.\n\n*Hint: A table of values of ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ for different values of $p$ is given below, where $p=Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $p$ | $\\,0.900\\,$ | $\\,0.950\\,$ | $\\,0.975\\,$ | $\\,0.990\\,$ | $\\,0.995\\,$ | $\\,0.999\\,$ | $\\,0.9995\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ | $\\,1.2816\\,$ | $\\,1.6449\\,$ | $\\,1.9600\\,$ | $\\,2.3263\\,$ | $\\,2.5758\\,$ | $\\,3.0902\\,$ | $\\,3.2905\\,$ |",
      "worked_solution": "Let $X$ represent the volume (in ounces) of soda in a can. Then we have $X∼N(12,{\\sigma }^{2}),$ and we want to find the standard deviation $\\sigma .$ \n\nWe know that $P(X\\le 11.5)=0.01.$ Converting $X$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 11.5) & =0.01 \\\\ P(Z\\le \\frac{11.5-12}{\\sigma }) & =0.01 \\\\ Φ(-\\frac{0.5}{\\sigma }) & =0.01 \\\\ -\\frac{0.5}{\\sigma } & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.01).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, \n\n$$\n{Φ}^{-1}(0.99)=2.3263,\n$$\n\nand therefore by symmetry, we have\n\n$$\n{Φ}^{-1}(1-0.99)={Φ}^{-1}(0.01)=-2.3263.\n$$\n\nFinally then, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{0.5}{\\sigma } & =-2.3263 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sigma } & =4.6524 \\\\ \\sigma  & =\\frac{1}{4.6524} \\\\  & ≈0.21.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
