    "q-95154": {
      "dom_id": "step-q95154",
      "name": "Question 15",
      "problem": "The duration of feline pregnancies is normally distributed with a mean of $\\mu $ days and a standard deviation of $5$ days. Additionally, $20%$ of the pregnancies have a duration lower than $59$ days. Find the mean $\\mu $, rounded to the nearest day.\n\n*Hint: A table of values of ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ for different values of $p$ is given below, where $p=Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $p$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.03\\,$ | $\\,0.05\\,$ | $\\,0.10\\,$ | $\\,0.15\\,$ | $\\,0.20\\,$ | $\\,0.25\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ | $\\,-2.3263\\,$ | $\\,-1.8808\\,$ | $\\,-1.6449\\,$ | $\\,-1.2816\\,$ | $\\,-1.0364\\,$ | $\\,-0.8416\\,$ | $\\,-0.6745\\,$ |",
      "worked_solution": "Let $X$ represent the duration of a randomly selected pregnancy. Then we have $X∼N(\\mu ,{5}^{2}),$ and we want to find the mean $\\mu .$ \n\nWe know that $P(X<59)=0.20.$ Converting $X$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<59) & =0.20 \\\\ P(Z\\le \\frac{59-\\mu }{5}) & =0.20 \\\\ Φ(\\frac{59-\\mu }{5}) & =0.20 \\\\ \\frac{59-\\mu }{5} & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.20).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, \n\n$$\n{Φ}^{-1}(0.20)=-0.8416.\n$$\n\nFinally then, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{59-\\mu }{5} & =-0.8416 \\\\ 59-\\mu  & =-4.208 \\\\ \\mu  & ≈63\\,\\text{days.}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
